WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.920
Between Hawaii and California,&nbsp;
--in an area about twice the size of Texas--

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.750
Is the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.250
For decades, tons of our plastic debris has
accumulated there because of swirling ocean

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:14.999
currents.

00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:18.980
It looks like a cloudy soup: and that’s
because the plastic objects are spaced far

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:25.230
apart, and they range in size from large debris
to microscopic.There are at least 4 other

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:30.300
garbage patches like this in the world, And
after scientists discovered them, starting

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.830
in the 90s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:36.450
They thought that this might be where a lot
of the plastic ended up, out there floating

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.730
on the surface.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:43.490
But recently, scientists brought large nets
to the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, and took

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.600
a closer look at the objects they pulled out.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:51.080
They found water bottles And hard hats
And bottle caps&nbsp;

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.010
And toothbrushes
And toilet seats

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.510
And laundry baskets
And using what they found, they were able

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.210
to calculate how much debris was in all of
the garbage patches.&nbsp;

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.360
There were about a few hundred thousand tons
of plastics at the surface of the ocean, which

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:06.550
is a huge number.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.510
It is a big number.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:13.860
But a few hundred thousand metric tons of
plastic is only about 1% of the estimated

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.400
8 million tonnes of plastic scientists believe
is emitted into the ocean every year.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:25.850
So scientists have been left investigating
a mystery: Where does the rest of the ocean’s

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:32.020
plastic go?

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.550
&nbsp;
This is clue number 1 in the case of the missing

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:39.510
plastic: a sea floor sediment sample&nbsp;
It was taken from the bottom of the Santa

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.659
Barbara Basin, off the coast of California.&nbsp;

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:48.960
It represents a measure of time, from 1870
at its deepest layer of sediment, up until

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:49.960
2009.&nbsp;

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:55.590
But this period, from 1945 to 2009, is where
the study authors were focused on.&nbsp;

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.840
It’s the era of plastic production.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.729
In these layers, the study authors found plastic
fibers and fragments that were 1 millimeter

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:06.020
or smaller in size.&nbsp;

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:12.180
They found more and more plastic particles
as the years went on, doubling every 15 years.&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.379
That rate is nearly identical to the rate
of global plastic production.&nbsp;

00:02:16.379 --> 00:02:21.519
Ss soon as you see a layer of microplastic
you pass the 1950’s and that's the legacy

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:23.219
of our generation.

00:02:23.219 --> 00:02:27.969
This is Laurent Lebreton—he works at the
Ocean Cleanup, and led the study of the objects

00:02:27.969 --> 00:02:29.859
in the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.

00:02:29.859 --> 00:02:32.480
And this will be there forever.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.099
We know that.&nbsp;

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:39.870
The sea sediment study looked at microplastics—particles
smaller than 5 millimeters.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:45.489
These either come from clothing fibers, or
they are the result of larger plastics breaking

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:46.489
down.&nbsp;

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:50.870
We’ve found these tiny particles floating
throughout the ocean, And even in the guts

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.480
of the ocean’s tiniest creatures, like plankton.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.079
But the sediment study shows that&nbsp;
some of our plastic is likely hiding, buried

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:02.059
in the sea floor, too.

00:03:02.059 --> 00:03:11.329
But here’s another clue: This is a plastic
bag, captured 2,500 meters below the surface

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:13.249
of the Arctic deep sea.

00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:18.629
It’s one of over 2,100 photographs taken
with this deep sea camera.&nbsp;

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:25.689
Part of the work that we're doing is to look
at the impact of climate change in the Arctic.

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:34.799
We use towed camera surveys to look at the
impact on large animals like starfish and

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:35.799
snails, sponges.

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:41.200
And while I was doing these surveys, I saw
that more and more plastic debris was on the

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.739
sea floor.

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:47.620
Melanie Bergmann’s research shows that large
plastic objects don’t just float on the

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:54.089
surface or degrade into microplastic — some
of them sink without breaking down.&nbsp;

00:03:54.089 --> 00:03:59.959
One study found that about 50% of plastic
in landfills is more dense than seawater,

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:02.409
which means these objects could sink on their
own.

00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:09.129
But even those other 50 percent may actually
travel to the sea floor with time, because

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:15.049
what we see is that the debris which is floating
on the ocean surface becomes colonized with

00:04:15.049 --> 00:04:20.660
biota over time… barnacles, mussels, all
sorts of different organisms… then it becomes

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:25.970
heavier and heavier and at certain at a certain
point and then starts to sink.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.099
Bergmann’s research is difficult to replicate
throughout the ocean, because of the challenge

00:04:30.099 --> 00:04:32.069
of surveying the deep sea environments.

00:04:32.069 --> 00:04:39.130
But it suggests that some of that missing
plastic might be sitting on the seafloor,

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.009
intact.

00:04:41.009 --> 00:04:45.900
Another clue complicates the mystery, though:
this plastic crate, from Taiwan.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.380
It’s one of the objects excavated during
that harvest in the Great Pacific Garbage

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:51.380
Patch.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:56.590
And what struck researchers was its production
date: 1971.&nbsp;

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:00.810
When they looked at the production dates of
other objects, they saw a trend: a lot of

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.139
it was old trash.

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:08.569
This was a new lead: because if the majority
of plastic pollution degraded into microplastic

00:05:08.569 --> 00:05:14.120
or fell to the ocean floor, then what you’d
see in garbage patches would be new plastic.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:19.139
And that changed the story...the plastic that
is accumulated at the surface of the ocean

00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:21.930
is actually very persistent.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:30.919
The plastic we find in subtropical oceans
may actually be there for decades, if not

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:32.490
centuries.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:37.851
It turns out the new plastic is far closer
than the open ocean or the bottom of the deep

00:05:37.851 --> 00:05:39.620
sea.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:45.030
Lebreton’s research found that plastic objects
on coastlines have more recent production

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.669
dates than plastic in the open ocean.&nbsp;

00:05:47.669 --> 00:05:51.720
This clue led scientists to think a lot of
debris actually stays close to shorelines

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.750
around the world—hidden in plain sight.&nbsp;

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:58.610
Some of that will end up in the middle of
the ocean and garbage patch, but actually

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:03.410
a lot of it stays fairly near shore and hop
from beach to beach to beach.&nbsp;

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:08.110
Erik Van Sebille is an oceanographer, and
is building an ocean model that predicts where

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.699
our missing plastic ends up.&nbsp;

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:15.509
The completed model will be finished in 2022,
but in the meantime he and his team publish

00:06:15.509 --> 00:06:17.900
initial results to a Twitter feed.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.618
We use simulations of the ocean currents and
they're a bit like weather models for the

00:06:21.618 --> 00:06:22.618
ocean.

00:06:22.618 --> 00:06:23.618
So they tell us how the currents are moving
stuff around.

00:06:23.618 --> 00:06:24.618
And then we put in virtual plastic…

00:06:24.618 --> 00:06:27.479
And then we move that plastic with the ocean
flow.

00:06:27.479 --> 00:06:33.050
At the same time the plastic and fragment,
it can degrade, organisms start growing on

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:38.600
it that weighs down the plastic so that it
slowly starts to sink into the deeper ocean.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:43.599
So in that way, we're doing like this gigantic
simulation of all of the ocean, of all the

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:45.770
plastic moving around.&nbsp;

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:52.129
Van Sebille thinks that a majority of plastic
pollution is within 100 miles of shorelines,&nbsp;

00:06:52.129 --> 00:06:56.909
continually getting washed back up on beaches,
down coastlines, or up and down to the sea

00:06:56.909 --> 00:06:57.909
floor.

00:06:57.909 --> 00:07:04.169
If the plastic continuously goes back and
forth between the coastline and offshore,

00:07:04.169 --> 00:07:09.219
that's a lot of rubbing and fragmenting and
scraping over the sand.

00:07:09.219 --> 00:07:16.159
This commotion helps explain the presence
of microplastics in sediments and animal guts,

00:07:16.159 --> 00:07:18.319
too.

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:24.620
Laurent’s organization is working on cleaning
up the garbage patches in the middle of the

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:25.620
ocean.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.490
But that won’t do much for the other 99%
of our plastic: The microplastics becoming

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.259
part of our food web and geologic record.

00:07:33.259 --> 00:07:35.969
The larger debris sinking to the bottom of
the ocean.

00:07:35.969 --> 00:07:40.469
And, more likely than not, getting washed
up on beaches.&nbsp;

00:07:40.469 --> 00:07:46.340
Like this one, where I recently visited, where
I found a mix of micro and larger plastics.&nbsp;

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:52.539
But knowing where plastics end up can help
us keep this plastic heap from growing.&nbsp;

00:07:52.539 --> 00:07:57.770
These photos were taken by volunteers at an
annual, international coastal clean-up event,

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:59.840
organized by the Ocean Conservancy.&nbsp;

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:03.370
Where people volunteer to pick up plastic
on beaches near them.&nbsp;

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:06.360
The most common objects they find are food
wrappers.&nbsp;

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.169
Cigarette butts.&nbsp;

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:11.939
Plastic bottle caps
And plastic cups and plates.&nbsp;

00:08:11.939 --> 00:08:16.310
The easiest way to get plastic like this out
of the ocean, is to prevent it from entering

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:17.949
in the first place.

00:08:17.949 --> 00:08:19.669
With better recycling programs.

00:08:19.669 --> 00:08:22.759
Or producing and using less plastic altogether.&nbsp;

00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:28.509
There are nearly 400,000 miles of coastline
around the world, not all of it accessible

00:08:28.509 --> 00:08:29.509
for people.&nbsp;

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:33.770
But, knowing that most of our plastic pollution
hangs out along shorelines before it becomes

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:39.200
microplastics or floats out to the open sea,
means beach clean-ups can go a long way in

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:42.160
preventing further damage.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:46.410
So if you see plastic pollution on a beach—all
the more reason to pick it up.

